1
00:00:00,900 --> 00:00:02,782
Here are tiny glowing beads,

2
00:00:02,822 --> 00:00:05,434
each one far smaller than the wavelength of light.

3
00:00:05,504 --> 00:00:07,577
Now look at them through a perfect microscope.

4
00:00:07,617 --> 00:00:11,822
Every bead has become a soft disc, wrapped in faint rings.

5
00:00:11,892 --> 00:00:14,568
Push two beads closer than about 200 nanometres,

6
00:00:14,608 --> 00:00:17,103
and they merge into a single blob.

7
00:00:17,173 --> 00:00:18,996
No lens maker can fix this.

8
00:00:19,036 --> 00:00:22,461
So why can't even a perfect lens make a perfect point?

9
00:00:25,681 --> 00:00:26,840
First, what is an image?

10
00:00:26,880 --> 00:00:30,583
Think of any object as a crowd of tiny points of light.

11
00:00:30,653 --> 00:00:33,293
A lens turns each point into the same small blur,

12
00:00:33,333 --> 00:00:36,619
called the point spread function, or PSF.

13
00:00:36,689 --> 00:00:40,243
Each point stamps a copy of the PSF at its own position,

14
00:00:40,283 --> 00:00:41,870
scaled by its brightness.

15
00:00:41,910 --> 00:00:45,693
The beads glow independently, so the stamps simply add.

16
00:00:45,763 --> 00:00:48,700
Adding up shifted copies like this is called a convolution.

17
00:00:48,740 --> 00:00:53,078
The image is the object, convolved with the PSF.

18
00:00:53,148 --> 00:00:56,099
Now feed the blur a single wave, a pattern of stripes.

19
00:00:56,139 --> 00:00:59,078
Out comes the same wave, only fainter.

20
00:00:59,148 --> 00:01:01,521
Just like propagation in episode one,

21
00:01:01,561 --> 00:01:04,384
waves are the eigenvectors of blurring.

22
00:01:04,424 --> 00:01:07,945
In the Fourier basis, convolution becomes multiplication.

23
00:01:08,015 --> 00:01:12,043
Each spatial frequency kx just gets multiplied by one number:

24
00:01:12,083 --> 00:01:16,555
the transfer function, the Fourier transform of the PSF.

25
00:01:16,625 --> 00:01:19,770
Notice how fine stripes fade much more than coarse ones.

26
00:01:20,590 --> 00:01:24,156
So where does the PSF come from? Recall episode one:

27
00:01:24,196 --> 00:01:26,770
any light field is a sum of plane waves.

28
00:01:26,840 --> 00:01:29,257
A point sends them out in every direction,

29
00:01:29,297 --> 00:01:34,991
each with its own kx = k sin θ. Together they fill the k-circle.

30
00:01:35,061 --> 00:01:37,403
But a lens has a finite opening.

31
00:01:37,443 --> 00:01:41,190
It only catches waves tilted less than some largest angle.

32
00:01:41,260 --> 00:01:44,427
The sine of that angle is the numerical aperture, NA.

33
00:01:44,467 --> 00:01:49,755
On the k-circle, the lens keeps just a cap: kx up to k times NA.

34
00:01:49,825 --> 00:01:54,794
Waves with kx beyond k are evanescent: they never even leave the object.

35
00:01:54,834 --> 00:01:58,330
So the lens is a hole in k-space, called the pupil.

36
00:01:58,400 --> 00:02:00,840
Add up only the waves that pass through this hole,

37
00:02:00,880 --> 00:02:05,451
and you get the PSF. Watch what happens as the aperture opens.

38
00:02:06,321 --> 00:02:10,047
The cap widens, and the spot shrinks. Its bright core,

39
00:02:10,087 --> 00:02:14,582
the Airy disc, has a radius of 0.61 λ/NA.

40
00:02:14,652 --> 00:02:16,320
And magnification can't save you:

41
00:02:16,360 --> 00:02:19,876
zooming in on the image only gives you a bigger blur.

42
00:02:20,696 --> 00:02:24,151
In 1873, Ernst Abbe asked a sharper question:

43
00:02:24,191 --> 00:02:27,454
what is the finest grating a lens can image?

44
00:02:27,524 --> 00:02:32,126
A grating of period d splits a plane wave into a straight

45
00:02:32,166 --> 00:02:36,946
beam and two first orders, tilted at sin θ = ±λ/d.

46
00:02:37,016 --> 00:02:40,759
The lens must catch the straight beam and at least one first order:

47
00:02:40,799 --> 00:02:43,262
their interference rebuilds the stripes.

48
00:02:43,332 --> 00:02:46,709
Now make the grating finer. The orders tilt further out,

49
00:02:46,749 --> 00:02:49,440
and once they leave the cap, the stripes are gone.

50
00:02:49,510 --> 00:02:54,556
So with straight-on light, the finest period a lens can image is λ/NA.

51
00:02:54,626 --> 00:02:56,279
Abbe's trick: tilt the illumination,

52
00:02:56,319 --> 00:02:59,278
so the straight beam enters at one edge of the cap.

53
00:02:59,318 --> 00:03:02,688
Now a first order fits in at the other edge.

54
00:03:03,558 --> 00:03:06,909
That doubles the reach, to a finest period of λ/2NA.

55
00:03:06,949 --> 00:03:09,560
This is the diffraction limit.

56
00:03:10,380 --> 00:03:13,222
Abbe's picture is for coherent light, like a laser,

57
00:03:13,262 --> 00:03:18,261
where fields add: the pupil cuts the field off sharply at k·NA.

58
00:03:18,331 --> 00:03:21,710
Glowing beads are different: each emits with its own random,

59
00:03:21,750 --> 00:03:25,575
ever-changing phase, so intensities add, not fields.

60
00:03:25,645 --> 00:03:27,989
Then the transfer function, called the OTF,

61
00:03:28,029 --> 00:03:32,108
is the overlap of the pupil with a shifted copy of itself.

62
00:03:32,178 --> 00:03:35,963
Slide one copy across the other. The overlap shrinks smoothly,

63
00:03:36,003 --> 00:03:39,518
and only reaches zero at twice the coherent cutoff.

64
00:03:39,588 --> 00:03:42,883
So incoherent light reaches twice as far in kx.

65
00:03:42,923 --> 00:03:47,174
But it isn't simply better: its contrast fades toward the cutoff.

66
00:03:47,244 --> 00:03:49,543
Here's one test chart, imaged both ways.

67
00:03:49,583 --> 00:03:51,835
Coherent light rings at every edge;

68
00:03:51,875 --> 00:03:56,024
incoherent light is smooth, but its finest spokes wash out.

69
00:03:56,844 --> 00:04:01,388
On a computer, imaging is the same three steps as in episode one:

70
00:04:01,428 --> 00:04:05,772
FFT, multiply by the OTF, inverse FFT.

71
00:04:05,842 --> 00:04:08,426
So can we run it backwards? Take a blurred image

72
00:04:08,466 --> 00:04:12,109
with a little camera noise, and divide by the OTF.

73
00:04:12,979 --> 00:04:15,631
Disaster. Wherever the OTF is tiny,

74
00:04:15,671 --> 00:04:19,870
the noise gets multiplied by thousands, and swamps everything.

75
00:04:19,940 --> 00:04:22,280
The Wiener filter is the stable version.

76
00:04:22,320 --> 00:04:25,305
It divides where the signal beats the noise,

77
00:04:25,345 --> 00:04:27,144
and gently gives up where it doesn't.

78
00:04:27,214 --> 00:04:30,608
But beyond the cutoff, the OTF is exactly zero.

79
00:04:30,648 --> 00:04:33,053
Those details never reached the camera,

80
00:04:33,093 --> 00:04:35,039
and no filter can bring them back.

81
00:04:35,109 --> 00:04:38,839
Only prior knowledge or extra measurements can fill them in.

82
00:04:38,879 --> 00:04:41,697
And that's where computational imaging begins.

83
00:04:42,517 --> 00:04:47,163
So here is the whole idea. Imaging is convolution with the PSF.

84
00:04:47,233 --> 00:04:51,157
A lens only keeps directions with sin θ ≤ NA,

85
00:04:51,197 --> 00:04:55,051
so details finer than about λ/2NA never arrive.

86
00:04:55,121 --> 00:04:59,153
One honest caveat: real lenses blur differently away from the centre,

87
00:04:59,193 --> 00:05:02,739
so the PSF slowly changes across the field.

88
00:05:02,809 --> 00:05:05,256
And we saw that intensities add for glowing beads.

89
00:05:05,296 --> 00:05:09,051
But why? When does light interfere, and when doesn't it?

